  <h3>5.1 For Educators &amp; Curriculum Planners</h3>
  <ul class="insight-list">
    <li><strong>Prioritize C Programming fundamentals:</strong> Across all 11 years, C language concepts (functions, loops, data types, operators, control structures) account for the largest share of keyword mentions. Ensure the syllabus allocates proportional time to these areas. Students who master C syntax, loops, and functions will be prepared for the majority of the paper.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't neglect DBMS theory:</strong> Database topics are the second-largest domain and show <strong>increasing emphasis</strong> over time. The consistent appearance of keys (primary, foreign), relationships, and MS-Access objects suggests these are considered core competency areas by examiners.</li>
    <li><strong>Prepare for volume variability:</strong> The year-over-year analysis shows that total question counts can fluctuate significantly. Students should be trained for flexibility — practicing both high-volume MCQ sets ({int(yearly_type_counts['MCQs'].max())} questions) and condensed papers ({int(yearly_type_counts['MCQs'].min())} questions) to avoid being caught off-guard.</li>
    <li><strong>Program-writing practice is essential:</strong> Short and Long questions frequently ask students to write C programs. This skill cannot be crammed — it requires sustained practice across topics (loops, conditionals, functions, arrays).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5.2 For Students</h3>
  <ul class="insight-list">
    <li><strong>MCQ preparation:</strong> Focus on factual recall — data type sizes, format specifiers, operator types, DBMS terminology. The MCQs consistently test these discrete knowledge items.</li>
